<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tomas Masaryk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk (1850–1937) was a Czech philosopher and politician who led the campaign abroad for Czechoslovak independence during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. He became the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1918 and was re-elected three times before retiring in 1935, shaping the country's democratic and liberal political culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk.jpg" alt="Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom%C3%A1%C5%A1_Garrigue_Masaryk_1925.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Masaryk was born on 7 March 1850 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodonin">Hodonín</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, to a poor, working-class family. His father, Jozef Masárik, was a Slovak carter who became steward and coachman at the imperial estate; his mother, Teresie Kropáčková, was a Moravian cook who had received a German education. He grew up in Čejkovice and attended grammar school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from 1865 to 1872.</p><p>He studied under Franz Brentano at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> and took his doctorate there in 1876 with a thesis on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> he met Charlotte Garrigue, an American from Brooklyn, whom he married on 15 March 1878; he took her family name as his middle name. He became professor of philosophy at the Czech Charles-Ferdinand University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1882, where his students included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a>. He challenged the supposedly medieval Czech epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and spoke against the blood libel in the 1899 Hilsner trial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Masaryk sat in the Austrian Reichsrat from 1891 to 1893 with the Young Czech Party and from 1907 to 1914 for the Czech Progressive Party, which he had founded in 1900. At first he favoured restructuring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> as a federal state. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> broke out he concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks should seek independence, and in December 1914 he went into exile with his daughter Olga.</p><p>He lectured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, wrote memoranda, and in January 1917 sent President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> a first memorandum on an independent Czechoslovak state. He helped organise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-legion">Czechoslovak Legion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which grew to about 40,000 men by 1918 after its performance at the Battle of Zborov in July 1917. In 1918 he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he had lectured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> in 1902 and 1907; on 18 October 1918 he submitted the Washington Declaration of Czechoslovak independence to Wilson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> recognised Masaryk as head of the provisional government, and on 14 November 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> elected him president while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He was re-elected in 1920, 1927 and 1934; the 1920 constitution limited presidents to two terms, but a one-time provision exempted the first holder. The constitution gave the president a limited role, but with ten or more parties in the Assembly and frequent changes of government, his prestige gave the office more influence than its framers intended. He built an informal political network known as the Hrad, the Castle.</p><p>He resigned on 14 December 1935 because of old age and poor health and was succeeded by Beneš. He died at Lány on 14 September 1937, at 87, and was buried beside his wife. Masaryk University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomáš Masaryk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its case had been made abroad by <b>Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</b>, Edvard Beneš and the Slovak astronomer and general Milan Rastislav Štefánik, whose Czechoslovak National Council in Paris won Allied recognition in 1918, helped by the fame of the Czechoslovak Legion, which had fought its way along the Trans-Siberian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mendel's work in Brno became a foundation of genetics after its rediscovery in 1900; <b>Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</b>, first president of Czechoslovakia, was born in Hodonín in 1850 and Sigmund Freud in Příbor in 1856; and the composer Leoš Janáček made his career in Brno.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its origins date to 1846, when Karel Havlíček Borovský first proposed it; Palacký refined it into a provisional political programme and <b>Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</b> completed it in New Europe: Slavic Viewpoint.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-slavism">Pan-Slavism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodonin">Hodonín</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in Lány Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.85,17.13&amp;year=1906" aria-label="Hodonín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4946 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5396 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5396 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.85,17.13&amp;year=1906">What was here in 1906? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
